# `remove-restrictions` / `unlock` / `change-permissions`, judged by other people's readers

Four tool settings — `remove-restrictions`, `unlock`, `change-permissions action: set`,
`change-permissions action: clear` — were run over the nine outside-locked fixtures in
`tests/fixtures/encrypted/` plus `plain.pdf` through the shipped `runPdfTool`, and the
40 PDFs they produced are committed in `docs/research/remove-restrictions-produced/`.
Everything below is what the readers make of those files:

    python3 docs/research/remove-restrictions-readers.py \
      docs/research/remove-restrictions-produced tests/fixtures/encrypted

so this log is the committed bytes' own output and can be re-derived, not a
transcription of a temp directory. The harness that pressed the buttons was a scratch
vitest file, deleted after the run like the flatten probe before it; the pin that
replaces it is `tests/pdf/remove-restrictions.test.ts`.

Nothing in this file is this app's opinion. pypdf 6.14.2, MuPDF 1.28.2, libqpdf 10.9.1
(through pikepdf) and poppler 26.10.0 each open the produced file with **no secret
typed** — "the password was removed" is only evidence when nothing is typed — and the
fixtures at the bottom are then opened with their own secret, so the before-picture is
measured on the same terms.

Columns: `MuPDF asks` is whether a reader has to be given anything to open the file;
`MuPDF /P` is its view of the permission flags, and **-4 means every bit set, i.e.
nothing restricted**; `planted` counts the sheets still carrying
`Lighthouse Inventory 1887`; `sheet-1 ink` is the non-white share of a 72 dpi raster,
against the fixture's own 1.51 %; `libqpdf check` is qpdf's own syntax check;
`/Encrypt in bytes` and `hunter2 in bytes` are greps of the downloaded file for the
trailer entry and for the typed secret in the clear.

